# Copy to .env. Never commit .env: it holds real credentials.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Two kinds of entry live here: the identity the containers run as, and the
# secrets referenced as env:NAME from adapters.toml.

# --- who the containers run as -------------------------------------------
#
# Without these the containers run as root and every file they write into
# ./state, ./target and ./.cache is owned by root, which means you need sudo to
# read your own roster or delete your own build directory. Fill them in with:
#
#   echo "HOST_UID=$(id -u)"
#   echo "HOST_GID=$(id -g)"
#   echo "DOCKER_GID=$(getent group docker | cut -d: -f3)"
#
# DOCKER_GID only matters if you use [provider.tor]: its hooks talk to
# the Docker socket, which is owned by the docker group. A web-only instance
# can leave it unset.
#
# These are per-machine. After moving this directory to another host, re-run
# the three commands above. Left unset here on purpose, so an .env copied from
# this file never claims a uid that does not exist on your machine.
#
# HOST_UID=1000
# HOST_GID=1000
# DOCKER_GID=989

# --- which compose files this instance is made of --------------------------
#
# The partyline pager is a base file plus one overlay per thing you run, so a
# Telegram instance never starts an ircd and never sees an XMPP password.
# Listing them here, colon-separated, is what lets a bare `docker compose up
# -d` in this directory mean the right thing; otherwise every command needs its
# own -f flags. `./partylinepager.sh` rewrites this line whenever you switch an
# adapter on or off.
#
# The base file is always first. Then one per network in adapters.toml, plus
# compose/docker-compose.tor.yml if policy.toml has [provider.tor], plus
# compose/docker-compose.apprise.yml if you want apprise: endpoints to work. The README
# section "Compose files" has a ready-made line for each combination.
#
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:compose/docker-compose.telegram.yml:compose/docker-compose.tor.yml

# --- adapter credentials --------------------------------------------------
#
# Only the ones you actually enabled in adapters.toml need a value.
#
# A value here does not reach the daemon on its own. Each one is passed in by
# that network's overlay file, so TELEGRAM_TOKEN is in the container only when
# you brought it up with -f compose/docker-compose.telegram.yml. Leaving a stale
# credential in this file is therefore inert rather than dangerous, but delete
# it anyway.
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
MATRIX_PASSWORD=
XMPP_PASSWORD=
MASTODON_TOKEN=
IMAP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
IRC_PASSWORD=
MATTERMOST_TOKEN=
DISCORD_TOKEN=

# --- transport directory overrides ----------------------------------------
#
# Default: transports/ dirs (published images, no checkout needed).
# To use a local git checkout instead, uncomment and adjust:
#
# TOR_PARTYLINE_DIR=${PWD}/party-lines/tor-party-line
# I2P_PARTYLINE_DIR=${PWD}/party-lines/i2p-party-line
# RETICULUM_PARTYLINE_DIR=${PWD}/party-lines/reticulum-party-line
